# Background Remover is cross-origin isolated on purpose. COOP and COEP are what
# a document needs before the browser hands it SharedArrayBuffer, and without
# SharedArrayBuffer ONNX Runtime's WASM build falls back to a single thread no
# matter how many cores the visitor has.
#
# EXACTLY ONE RULE MAY MATCH THIS PAGE. `/tools/background-remover/*` already
# covers the trailing-slash URL. Adding `/tools/background-remover/` as well
# makes both rules match, each policy is then sent twice, and the joined value
# `same-origin, same-origin` is not a valid value — the browser falls back to
# unsafe-none and the page is not isolated at all. That exact mistake shipped
# once and hid the real problem below for weeks, because the page looked
# configured while quietly staying on the slow path.
#
# The history, since this route has been broken by these headers before:
#
#   1. The headers went out duplicated, so isolation never took effect. Nobody
#      noticed, because the tool kept working on the single-threaded path.
#   2. The duplicate was removed. Isolation took effect for the first time, the
#      runtime took its multi-threaded path for the first time, and the tool
#      broke outright on the owner's sixteen-core machine. The headers were
#      pulled and the route went back to single-threaded.
#   3. The threaded path was then reproduced and measured. The defect was the
#      thread count: the library asks ONNX Runtime for one thread per logical
#      core and exposes no override, and past a couple of threads it loses to
#      contention badly. Measured on four cores, one image: 17.9s at one thread,
#      8.8s at two, 11.6s at four, 25.9s at sixteen. A sixteen-core visitor was
#      asking for the slowest setting available — worse than not threading.
#
# So `plannedThreadCount` now caps the request (sixteen cores ask for four), and
# a threaded attempt that fails for any reason other than the user canceling is
# retried automatically on a single thread, which makes the worst case the speed
# the tool had before rather than a broken tool.
#
# Verified on the sixteen-core machine that originally broke, with isolation
# genuinely in effect (`crossOriginIsolated` true, `hardwareConcurrency` 16):
# inference 5.98s against the 24.2s that machine took single-threaded, upload to
# output, no fallback triggered.
#
# If this ever regresses, `AGENTS.md` has the procedure for reproducing
# isolation locally — neither `astro dev` nor `astro preview` sends these, so
# this file cannot be tested through either of them.
/tools/background-remover/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
